---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
---

<Base title="Install" description="Install EchoPBX with Docker in a few minutes.">
  <section class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-slate-50">
    <div class="wrap py-12">
      <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Install EchoPBX</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-lg text-slate-600">
        EchoPBX runs as a single Docker container. This takes a few minutes on any Linux server.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="wrap prose-page max-w-3xl pb-20 text-slate-700">
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-md border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-amber-900">
      EchoPBX is currently in beta. If you run into trouble, please
      <a href="https://github.com/LJFloor/EchoPBX/issues">open an issue</a>. No question is too small.
    </div>

    <h2>Requirements</h2>
    <p>
      A Linux machine with Docker. Asterisk does not run on Windows, but you can use
      <a href="https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/">Docker Desktop</a> to run the container there.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Install Docker</h2>
    <p>On Ubuntu-based distributions:</p>
    <CodeBlock code="sudo apt install docker.io" />

    <h2>2. Run the container</h2>
    <CodeBlock code="sudo docker run -d --name echopbx --restart unless-stopped --network=host -v /opt/echopbx:/data ghcr.io/ljfloor/echopbx:main" />
    <p>
      The container runs in the background and starts again after a reboot. To see its logs, run
      <code>sudo docker logs -f echopbx</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      All data is stored in <code>/opt/echopbx</code> on the host. The container uses host networking,
      which exposes SIP on port 5060 and the web interface on port 8740. Publishing those ports in bridge
      mode caused connection and one-way audio problems with some phones, so host networking is the
      recommended setup for now.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Open the web interface</h2>
    <p>
      Browse to <code>http://&lt;server-ip&gt;:8740</code>. The setup wizard asks you to accept the license
      and create an admin account. After that you can add extensions, trunks and queues.
    </p>

    <h2>Connect a phone</h2>
    <p>
      Any SIP client or IP phone works. For a softphone on your computer,
      <a href="https://www.microsip.org">MicroSIP</a> is easy to set up. The extension page in EchoPBX
      shows the settings to enter, and the phone appears as connected once it registers.
    </p>

    <h2>Updating</h2>
    <p>
      Pull the new image, remove the old container and start it again. Your data in <code>/opt/echopbx</code> is kept.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      code={[
        "sudo docker pull ghcr.io/ljfloor/echopbx:main",
        "sudo docker rm -f echopbx",
        "sudo docker run -d --name echopbx --restart unless-stopped --network=host -v /opt/echopbx:/data ghcr.io/ljfloor/echopbx:main",
      ].join("\n")}
    />

    <h2>Build from source</h2>
    <CodeBlock code="docker build -t echopbx ." />
    <p>
      To develop without Docker, see the
      <a href="https://github.com/LJFloor/EchoPBX#developing-locally">instructions in the README</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
